WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:09.940
FISHER: Good morning.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:10.940
I'm Peter Fisher.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.200
I teach at the Tuck School of Business at
Dartmouth.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:19.610
And when I was invited this morning to preside
over this panel, what came to mind was the

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:25.130
boyhood experience when my father was on sabbatical
in London, and my parents took my brother

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:30.880
and I to see the American comedian Danny Kaye
conduct the London Philharmonic Orchestra.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.169
(Laughter.)

00:00:32.169 --> 00:00:38.230
It was a one-gag evening, which is, the London
Philharmonic is so good, it doesn't need a

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:39.230
conductor.

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:40.230
(Laughter.)

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:43.399
Now, given who I have up with me on the stage,
I feel like Danny Kaye.

00:00:43.399 --> 00:00:44.399
(Laughter.)

00:00:44.399 --> 00:00:48.219
But I hope I'll be as good as he was at getting
out of the way.

00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:52.809
So, without further ado, you have everyone's
biographies in your book.

00:00:52.809 --> 00:00:58.399
But, briefly put, we have here Rana Foroohar,
the assistant managing editor of Time Magazine

00:00:58.399 --> 00:01:03.039
and the author of the new book, "Maker and
Takers: The Rise of the Finance and the Fall

00:01:03.039 --> 00:01:08.960
of American Business"; John Lipsky, former
first deputy managing director of the-something

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.720
called the International Monetary Fund-(laughter)-and
a senior fellow at the John(s) Hopkins School

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:19.140
of Advanced International Studies; and we
have Joseph Stiglitz, a university professor

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:21.370
at Columbia Business School.

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:25.110
And we're here to discuss "Is U.S. Finance
Hurting Growth?"

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:31.840
And I'm going to pose the same question to
each of you in sequence-I think Rana, Joe,

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:32.940
John.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:36.490
Do we have too much or too little finance?

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:39.140
Is it hurting us or helping us?

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.460
And over what horizon are you thinking about?

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:42.460
Rana?

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:43.460
FOROOHAR: OK, I'll kick it off.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:45.820
Well, first of all, let me say thank you for
having me here.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:48.890
I'm incredibly honored to be on a stage with
all of you.

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:52.350
I am a humble journalist and not a trained
economist.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:56.890
But I've been a business and economic journalist
for 23 years, and so my perspective in my

00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:00.700
book is really built off of that observation
and talking to very smart people like the

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:05.440
ones on stage about what's going on in the
global economy.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:10.479
So I began working on my book about three
years ago in the wake of the financial crisis

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:15.250
because I was trying to understand why there
was still such an incredible divide between

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:18.810
Wall Street and Main Street, and in particular
where the markets were, and where the real

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.640
economy was, and what was that divide about.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:25.920
I was going and, you know, talking to companies
and seeing individuals in various parts of

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:29.980
the country, and they just simply were not
feeling the recovery in the same way that

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:31.780
the markets were perceiving them.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:38.500
So I began digging into it-the research, essentially,
on finance and growth.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:43.390
And what I came to find was that I think the
nature of the financial system in the U.S.

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:45.910
has changed profoundly over the last 40 years.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:50.280
I actually do in my book a kind of a hundred-year
overview, because I wanted to compare the

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:55.020
run-up to the Great Depression with the period
in the run-up to the subprime crisis.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:59.750
But I would say that the period of what I
call "financialization," the modern period,

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:02.590
started about 40 years ago.

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:08.250
And during that time, the size of the financial
sector as a percentage of the economy has

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:09.250
almost quadrupled.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:13.760
So it's gone from 2 1/2 (percent), 3 percent,
to close to 8 percent.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.860
But during that time, trend growth, of course,
has not grown.

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:24.819
And there have been a number of metrics about
number of startups falling, entrepreneurial

00:03:24.819 --> 00:03:31.040
zeal going down by a variety of measures,
R&amp;D spending going down relative to, say,

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.989
share buybacks in companies, that show that
business has actually suffered as finance

00:03:35.989 --> 00:03:36.989
has gotten bigger.

00:03:36.989 --> 00:03:40.099
And there's a lot of studies-it's all cited
in the books, so-and there's smart people

00:03:40.099 --> 00:03:44.380
who've done some of these studies on the-on
the stage here, so I won't go into them.

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:49.800
But basically, by a lot of metrics, the size
of finance in the U.S. has started to slow

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:50.800
growth.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:54.130
And, in fact, there's some studies, BIS studies
and others, that show that that happens actually

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:58.490
when finance is just half the size that it
is in the U.S. right now.

00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:01.910
But then the other thing that I would mention-and
I was very interested as I began to speak

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:07.239
to different academics about this-is the nature
of change in what the financial sector does.

00:04:07.239 --> 00:04:12.560
So, if you go back to kind of, you know, the
original nature of what the capital markets

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.530
were supposed to do to business, they're supposed
to take everybody's savings, you put it in

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:19.340
financial institutions, the financial institutions
lend that money out to business, business

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:20.750
grows and creates jobs.

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:27.650
Actually, there are some deep academic studies
that show about only 15 percent of all the

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:32.050
financial flows in the U.S. financial institutions
right now are going to business lending.

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:37.520
The rest of it is basically trading against
existing assets-stocks, bonds, houses, the

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:40.910
real estate market being a large part of that,
of course.

00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:45.440
And then so I try and get into the ramifications
of this for business in a variety of chapters

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:46.830
in my book.

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:51.070
And I'll just leave one last stat before I
pass the baton here.

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:54.780
One of the fascinating things that I found-and
I explored this through stories of a number

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:59.610
of companies-is that business itself has come
to emulate finance.

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:04.500
So, if you look at the amount of revenue that
businesses across all industries are getting

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:11.680
now from financial services, from financial
tactics like tax optimization, that's increased

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:13.440
fivefold since the 1980s.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:15.720
So it's almost as though we've all become
bankers.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:21.580
And finance has become, in my view, the tail
that wags the dog for industry, rather than

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:22.810
the other way around.

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:24.030
So I'll stop there.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:25.090
FISHER: Thank you.

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:27.770
Joe, too much, too little, or the wrong mix?

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:32.729
STIGLITZ: Well, first let me say Rana's book
is fantastic, so I'd encourage all of you-she

00:05:32.729 --> 00:05:34.780
can't say that, but I can say that.

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:35.780
(Laughter.)

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:41.860
So it is really interesting.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:47.300
The basic perspective that Rana put, that
there's been this enormous increase in the

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:55.800
relative size of the financial sector to GDP
without any evidence that this has led to

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:00.580
any benefit to economic growth, there are
actually two other aspects of this that I'd

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:03.300
just mention briefly.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:12.040
Looking across countries, one is that-a study
that I did with Bill Easterly when I was chief

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:21.630
economist of the World Bank, which showed
that, if you look at instability, as you increase,

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:27.580
there's a-there's a U-shaped curve.

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:33.720
Some degree of finance is very important-diversifies
risk, so it's an important function.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:37.650
But if you look across countries, when it
gets too big, there's more instability.

00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:44.759
And, of course, instability is bad not only
for growth, but for a whole other set of effects.

00:06:44.759 --> 00:06:51.300
The second thing that-Jamie Galbraith has
a nice book that describes, is there's a systematic

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:58.340
relationship between the degree of financialization
and inequality.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:03.690
And for a whole variety of reasons, not a
surprise.

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:09.070
The real question, though, is, when you start
looking at the micro-not the macro, but look

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:14.850
underneath-and you ask the question, what
is the financial sector doing and, like Rana

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:17.639
said, where are they making money?

00:07:17.639 --> 00:07:25.850
It turns out a very large fraction of their-of
their revenues come not from the core activities

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:32.699
that we model the financial sector-you know,
that lending activity, that risk mitigation,

00:07:32.699 --> 00:07:41.530
those kinds of things that are core-but from
all kinds of, you might say, antisocial activities-(laughter)-like

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:47.130
LIBOR manipulation, forex manipulation.

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:49.850
I don't want-you know, hard to quantify.

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:51.660
Some of, though, you-has been quantified.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:57.389
You ask questions like using market power
over the medium of exchange, the rents they

00:07:57.389 --> 00:08:06.060
extract in interchange fees are very significant.

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:09.870
And that's where the big-you know, there's
been a lot-an attempt to curb it a little

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:13.910
bit in Dodd-Frank, but the Fed really missed
it.

00:08:13.910 --> 00:08:19.410
And the Dodd-Frank only focuses on debit cards,
not-didn't do anything about credit cards.

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:27.259
Schiller and Akerlof had this very nice book
called "Phishing for Phools," where they use

00:08:27.259 --> 00:08:35.240
the word-"ph," picking up on the internet-that
a very large part of the revenue comes from

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:39.310
trying to find people that they can take advantage
of.

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:41.599
And there are a lot of fools out there.

00:08:41.599 --> 00:08:47.490
And you might say, globalization opened up
a global market for fools, and they were very

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:49.630
good at finding them.

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:53.450
You know, a little village in Norway or, you
know.

00:08:53.450 --> 00:09:08.450
So that contrast with another study that's
cited in Rana's book, which is that the efficiency

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:13.760
of the sector in providing the services that
it does, you would have thought the cost would

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:17.670
have gone down because it's basically a tech
sector, you know?

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:20.140
It's processing data.

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:24.981
And our technology has been-you know, Moore's
Law has brought down that cost-doesn't show

00:09:24.981 --> 00:09:29.930
up in the cost that they charge for those
kinds of services.

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:36.390
So that comes to the question that you posed,
Peter, which is, the question isn't just the

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:42.480
size of the financial sector, that it's doing
too much of what it shouldn't and not enough

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:44.380
of what it should do.

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:51.430
And then this comes to the core regulatory
issues that we've been debating since the

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:53.290
financial crisis.

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:59.800
Most of what the debate has been focused on
is stopping the financial sector from imposing

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:02.890
harms on the rest of the society.

00:10:02.890 --> 00:10:07.230
Very little of it has been addressed to the
question, how do we actually make the financial

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:10.490
sector do what we hope it's supposed to do?

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:11.870
And which is very important.

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:15.420
So, you know, I'm very much in favor of the
financial sector.

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:18.170
I think the financial sector is really important.

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:24.380
The real problem is it hasn't been doing as
much-you know, it's been diverted from performing

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:26.910
those core functions.

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:33.440
And just one closing remark is, you know,
there's a-you might-you may say almost a personal

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:41.050
aspect of this, of how it adversely affects
our growth.

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:47.060
A disproportionately large fraction of our
best students, you know, and my best students,

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:54.610
wind up in finance, for good reason; it's
where the money is, and they're reasonably

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:57.040
rational.

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:02.232
But one would have hoped that there would
have been more diversification, more of them

00:11:02.232 --> 00:11:11.810
would have gone into other areas of inquiry-you
know, public service, research, you know.

00:11:11.810 --> 00:11:17.350
So I think one of the reasons why it has not
had the positive effect that you would have

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:23.650
hoped, I mean, is that it has distorted the
structure of our economies.

00:11:23.650 --> 00:11:28.590
And not only is the financial sector distorted,
there's a distortion in the whole structure

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:29.590
of our-of our economy.

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:31.490
FISHER: Thank you, Joe.

00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:34.820
John, too much, too little, what horizon?

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:36.140
LIPSKY: (Laughs.)

00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:41.279
Well, I've chosen to interpret this question
a little more narrowly, but before that just

00:11:41.279 --> 00:11:48.670
say Joe is an old friend and absolutely brilliant,
but he obviously doesn't work in a financial

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:50.860
institution.

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:53.040
(Laughter.)

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:59.670
So he hasn't had to watch his margins shrink,
he hasn't had to watch the onslaught of new

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:05.540
regulation, he hasn't had to deal with the
historically unprecedented monetary policy

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:12.510
that has produced a very adverse environment
for profitability, and he hasn't had to worry

00:12:12.510 --> 00:12:18.760
about all the hotshot young grads heading
off to Silicon Valley to join fintech firms

00:12:18.760 --> 00:12:24.760
that are-certain are going to cannibalizing
the evil banks.

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:31.810
And it seems to me, when you're thinking about
this, the-two aspects of this conversation

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:33.610
that need to be kept in mind.

00:12:33.610 --> 00:12:39.800
The last 40 years, or 60 years, since World
War II, has seen a dramatic transformation

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:47.160
of the global economy that has produced fantastic
and unprecedented challenges.

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:54.950
And this is an-I tend to, when I hear about
this in the financial sector, I don't think

00:12:54.950 --> 00:13:00.450
the most powerful forces have been created
by the financial sector; I would say have

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:02.610
been reflected in the financial sector.

00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:05.950
Of course, there's a-this is simultaneous,
there's feedback.

00:13:05.950 --> 00:13:12.149
But I don't think that the most powerful forces
have come from the financial sector; quite

00:13:12.149 --> 00:13:14.149
the-quite the other way around.

00:13:14.149 --> 00:13:20.459
And in that context, the notion that let's
think about national markets has becoming

00:13:20.459 --> 00:13:21.700
progressively less relevant.

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:27.980
And yet, so much of the discussion has been
around national markets, and so much of the

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:34.089
response to the crisis has been by national
regulators and authorities to try to ring

00:13:34.089 --> 00:13:39.620
fence their market, such that international
markets have become less efficient and less

00:13:39.620 --> 00:13:41.910
effective over time.

00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:49.330
So I've interpreted the question, the topic
today, to ask, have we-post-crisis, we clearly

00:13:49.330 --> 00:13:55.829
faced a challenge on, one, restoring global
growth; number two, improving financial-sector

00:13:55.829 --> 00:14:01.200
stability so we never saw another global financial
crisis like we just had.

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:05.880
It's quite remarkable, seven or eight years
on from the onset of the crisis, depending

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:12.200
on how you want to time it, this regulatory
reform is still a work in progress.

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:18.010
And some of the most powerful measures have
yet to-yet to be enacted.

00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:19.010
Quite remarkable.

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:24.980
Even Dodd-Frank, which has produced about
22,000 pages of legislation and regulation,

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:27.610
is still incomplete.

00:14:27.610 --> 00:14:35.420
And the changes in regulation have forced
very costly action on financial institutions

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:42.490
that-and I would say, if there's a criticism,
not that there wasn't-that change wasn't necessary,

00:14:42.490 --> 00:14:48.880
but I would say that the-there has been insufficient
attention given to the overall impact to a

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:54.769
whole series of regulatory and legal measures
that were taken in a little bit of a-and I

00:14:54.769 --> 00:15:00.810
don't want to sound condescending, but a little
bit of a whac-a-mole kind of a manner.

00:15:00.810 --> 00:15:02.829
Here was a problem, whack.

00:15:02.829 --> 00:15:04.459
Here's another problem, whack.

00:15:04.459 --> 00:15:08.350
Is anybody standing back and saying, what
are we doing to the system?

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:09.399
I don't think so.

00:15:09.399 --> 00:15:11.550
Here are two little examples.

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:19.570
Anti-money laundering, financing of terrorism:
we've decided that bankers and financial institutions

00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:21.380
need to also be policemen.

00:15:21.380 --> 00:15:27.220
Now that's all well and good-that's all well
and good, but a collateral damage has been

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:31.940
of the effort in anti-money laundering to
collapse correspondent banking relationships

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:39.830
worldwide, and deprived some of the most-some
of the poorest nations from access to modern

00:15:39.830 --> 00:15:40.830
banking.

00:15:40.830 --> 00:15:43.680
Did the folks designing this think about that?

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:45.839
I don't think so.

00:15:45.839 --> 00:15:51.990
Second, when we talk about the wonderful profitability
of the banking system, most of the large banks

00:15:51.990 --> 00:15:58.110
in the U.S. and in Europe trade today below
book value.

00:15:58.110 --> 00:16:07.050
That's hardly a recipe for producing-for supporting
growth, and supporting growth over time.

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:13.600
So I would say, have the-if the question is,
have the reforms so far hindered growth, which

00:16:13.600 --> 00:16:20.209
have-has the U.S. financial system hindered
growth, I would say, yes, this is true.

00:16:20.209 --> 00:16:24.340
At the same-relative to what it might have
been.

00:16:24.340 --> 00:16:27.990
Have it-has these changes, at the same time,
enhanced stability?

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:30.550
I would say, yes, they have.

00:16:30.550 --> 00:16:32.779
But do we have the tradeoff right?

00:16:32.779 --> 00:16:33.779
I'm not at all convinced.

00:16:33.779 --> 00:16:34.779
I'll stop there.

00:16:34.779 --> 00:16:41.130
FISHER: Let me pick on an early point John
made, and press Rana and Joe.

00:16:41.130 --> 00:16:43.800
Let's put aside front-running customers and
abusing.

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:47.110
Yes, that happens.

00:16:47.110 --> 00:16:52.280
But is it causation, or is it correlation,
or is it a secondary effect?

00:16:52.280 --> 00:16:55.410
We have 40 years of falling interest rates.

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:58.449
We squeeze-and the central banks of the world
squeezed inflation out of the system from

00:16:58.449 --> 00:17:00.230
1982 to 2002.

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:05.990
We're surprised that the value of holding
financial assets, it's a lucrative thing to

00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:09.980
be in the business of holding these things,
asset-based finance.

00:17:09.980 --> 00:17:14.970
And so we watched the financial sector grow
in size while you bring down the discount

00:17:14.970 --> 00:17:17.199
rate on all those future cash flows.

00:17:17.199 --> 00:17:21.559
Does that mean it caused the decline in growth?

00:17:21.559 --> 00:17:26.859
Robert Gordon is pretty gloomy, but I don't
recall-and I read every page of his book-him

00:17:26.859 --> 00:17:31.379
putting a lot of weight on the financial sector
as dragging down productivity.

00:17:31.379 --> 00:17:33.880
He's got plenty of other causes he's pointing
to.

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:39.609
So I see correlation, and I may see secondary
effect, but to say the financial sector got

00:17:39.609 --> 00:17:45.149
up in the morning and did it, putting aside
abusive client, is a little harder for me,

00:17:45.149 --> 00:17:47.179
even though I sort of see what you're driving
at.

00:17:47.179 --> 00:17:48.180
Let me press you both on that.

00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:49.180
FOROOHAR: Well, so, it's a really good point.

00:17:49.180 --> 00:17:50.180
And I would just say, and I mentioned this
in my book, that financialization, globalization,

00:17:50.180 --> 00:17:51.180
and technological job destruction are definitely
moving in tandem, and in some ways create

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:52.180
snowball cycles with each other.

00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:53.180
I mean, you know, one of the things I explore
is how in corporations, the CFO's office-I

00:17:53.180 --> 00:17:54.180
look at this topic culturally as well, just
to preface-the CFO's office, and the sort

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:55.180
of way in which corporate management has become
very balance-sheet oriented, leads to certain

00:17:55.180 --> 00:17:56.180
decisions around outsourcing, for example,
that a lot of companies are starting to reconsider

00:17:56.180 --> 00:17:57.180
now.

00:17:57.180 --> 00:17:58.180
You know, there is-there is a tremendous movement
to reshore and rethink the typical Finance

00:17:58.180 --> 00:17:59.180
101 teaching of, you know, marshal your capital,
you know, cut costs, worry about talent, worry

00:17:59.180 --> 00:18:00.180
about everything else later.

00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:01.180
I think that there's a big shift.

00:18:01.180 --> 00:18:02.180
And actually I think that that's for the reason
that you're talking about.

00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:03.180
The traditional business school teaching for
executives, which is very financially oriented,

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:04.180
is actually very out of date for the challenges
of the day, in part because we are awash in

00:18:04.180 --> 00:18:05.180
a world of capital.

00:18:05.180 --> 00:18:06.180
There's plenty of money sloshing around.

00:18:06.180 --> 00:18:07.180
And so making decisions based on that, which
is a very financialized way to make corporate

00:18:07.180 --> 00:18:08.180
decisions, actually doesn't work as well.

00:18:08.180 --> 00:18:09.180
And I have some great anecdotes from different
corporate leaders in my book about that.

00:18:09.180 --> 00:18:10.180
So I think causality is difficult to prove.

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:11.580
I'm also struck, though, and I'm remembering
Rajan's comment in his book.

00:18:11.580 --> 00:18:15.869
The central banker of India used to teach
at University of Chicago that "let them eat

00:18:15.869 --> 00:18:18.940
credit" could be sort of the mantra for the
last 40 years.

00:18:18.940 --> 00:18:21.299
And this goes along with exactly what you're
saying.

00:18:21.299 --> 00:18:26.549
Low interest rates, in some ways, policies
taken across different administrations, both

00:18:26.549 --> 00:18:32.139
Republican and Democratic, that mask underlying
growth problems with easy money and easy credit,

00:18:32.139 --> 00:18:35.100
and I see this as part of the process of financialization.

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:42.259
STIGLITZ: So, you know, obviously it's always
difficult to separate causation correlation,

00:18:42.259 --> 00:18:45.090
particularly there are many factors.

00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:52.049
So no one will say it is the factor that has
caused the slowdown in economic growth; it

00:18:52.049 --> 00:18:54.090
is one of the factors.

00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:58.570
But as one thinks about it, there are several
channels by which it has, both by what it's

00:18:58.570 --> 00:18:59.570
done and what it's not done, so let me talk
about it.

00:18:59.570 --> 00:19:00.570
One of them is I think it's unambiguous that
it has contributed, not only in the United

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:01.570
States, but in other countries, to more instability.

00:19:01.570 --> 00:19:02.570
That most people would say that it did play
a role in the buildup in the bubble that led

00:19:02.570 --> 00:19:03.570
to the crisis.

00:19:03.570 --> 00:19:04.570
Now, you could say it was low interest rates.

00:19:04.570 --> 00:19:05.570
But the financial sector was supposed to manage
risk, you know, it was not supposed to be

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:06.570
engaged in massive fraud.

00:19:06.570 --> 00:19:07.570
It was supposed to-you know, securitization
was about risk diversification well-managed,

00:19:07.570 --> 00:19:08.570
OK?

00:19:08.570 --> 00:19:09.570
It didn't happen.

00:19:09.570 --> 00:19:11.369
And so if you read, like, the report of the
Financial Inquiry Commission, very clear that

00:19:11.369 --> 00:19:15.580
it was some of the things that the financial
sector did and didn't do that played a very

00:19:15.580 --> 00:19:18.169
big role in the crisis of 2008.

00:19:18.169 --> 00:19:22.980
And there's no doubt that crises are bad for
economic growth.

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:25.070
They have long-term effects.

00:19:25.070 --> 00:19:30.000
We still are not really recovered from the
effects of the crisis.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:31.739
So that's one example.

00:19:31.739 --> 00:19:40.100
A second one is a broader-let me say really
the same thing that Rana did, in a slightly

00:19:40.100 --> 00:19:41.389
different language.

00:19:41.389 --> 00:19:42.710
What do CEOs focus on?

00:19:42.710 --> 00:19:45.350
What do corporations focus on?

00:19:45.350 --> 00:19:56.499
Making a better product or manipulating taxes
and deciding where to locate based on tax

00:19:56.499 --> 00:19:57.499
advantages?

00:19:57.499 --> 00:20:06.220
You know, that kind of financial management
versus innovation, real sector management.

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:12.009
And there's a, you know, a well-developed
theory about managerial attention being limited,

00:20:12.009 --> 00:20:15.269
and when you focus on one thing you're not
focusing on another.

00:20:15.269 --> 00:20:22.669
So the financialization of the economy more
broadly I think has diverted the attention

00:20:22.669 --> 00:20:29.039
of corporate America away from some of the
real things that would have increased productivity.

00:20:29.039 --> 00:20:35.919
And finally, it goes back, you know, again,
to pick up the word that Rana said.

00:20:35.919 --> 00:20:46.049
If you look at what we model the financial
sector as doing, which is taking money from

00:20:46.049 --> 00:20:56.169
savers, intermediating, and providing it to
firms that are going to lead to more economic

00:20:56.169 --> 00:20:59.320
growth, that's not what's been going on in
the country.

00:20:59.320 --> 00:21:04.899
In fact, there are these studies that show
that, on average, money is flowing out of

00:21:04.899 --> 00:21:06.629
the corporate sector.

00:21:06.629 --> 00:21:11.750
So its traditional role that we describe in
the textbook of intermediating between savers

00:21:11.750 --> 00:21:14.979
and investors isn't there.

00:21:14.979 --> 00:21:20.609
One strong way of looking at this from a global
point of view-you know, we're at the Council

00:21:20.609 --> 00:21:26.970
on Foreign Relations, so think about it from
a global point of view-is Ben Bernanke remember

00:21:26.970 --> 00:21:31.889
said that we had a savings glut.

00:21:31.889 --> 00:21:38.970
And when he said it, you know, from a perch
of somebody who does a lot of work in development

00:21:38.970 --> 00:21:44.530
and a lot of concern about climate change,
my reaction was he was living on a different

00:21:44.530 --> 00:21:51.289
planet than I was, that I looked around the
world and I said we need so much money for

00:21:51.289 --> 00:21:56.059
retrofitting the global economy, for climate
change, so much money for infrastructure.

00:21:56.059 --> 00:22:00.349
Even New York City we know has some problems.

00:22:00.349 --> 00:22:04.639
And so you look, say, look, we have a shortage
of finance.

00:22:04.639 --> 00:22:11.779
So you ask the question, how do you reconcile
his view with the view, you know, you open

00:22:11.779 --> 00:22:17.149
your eyes, you say we aren't financing the
investment we need.

00:22:17.149 --> 00:22:23.580
And the answer is, between those two is supposed
to be a financial sector, and particularly

00:22:23.580 --> 00:22:33.700
a lot of the savings is long term, pension
funds, sovereign wealth funds who are long-term

00:22:33.700 --> 00:22:34.700
horizon.

00:22:34.700 --> 00:22:39.220
The investment needs are long-term needs,
long-term infrastructure.

00:22:39.220 --> 00:22:45.419
But sitting between the two is a financial
sector, that is often looking at quarterly,

00:22:45.419 --> 00:22:52.679
daily, nanosecond, can't serve that need of
intermediating between long-term savers and

00:22:52.679 --> 00:22:53.679
long-term investors.

00:22:53.679 --> 00:23:01.309
So my view has been, you know, that there's
a real dysfunction here in its ability to

00:23:01.309 --> 00:23:04.169
do what it's supposed to do from a global
perspective.

00:23:04.169 --> 00:23:09.159
FISHER: Well, let me push back and then see
if John wants to pick up the mantle just for

00:23:09.159 --> 00:23:10.200
the fun of it.

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:11.200
(Laughter.)

00:23:11.200 --> 00:23:14.190
Ben Bernanke's savings glut struck me as the
wrong side of the coin.

00:23:14.190 --> 00:23:15.190
STIGLITZ: Yeah.

00:23:15.190 --> 00:23:19.100
FISHER: We had a glut of consumption and excessive
income here, which is how China ended up with

00:23:19.100 --> 00:23:23.909
a savings glut, and they financed our glut
of consumption and excessive income.

00:23:23.909 --> 00:23:25.570
It started here.

00:23:25.570 --> 00:23:29.470
And that was from the accommodative monetary
policy, stimulating the interest rate-sensitive

00:23:29.470 --> 00:23:32.369
sectors of the economy, housing and autos.

00:23:32.369 --> 00:23:36.429
And when housing and autos collapsed, oh,
dear, it wasn't the fault of monetary policy,

00:23:36.429 --> 00:23:41.359
it was the fault of something called financial
stability in the financial sector and washing

00:23:41.359 --> 00:23:46.639
its hands of it and saying, what, me, worry,
I didn't do that when, of course, accommodative

00:23:46.639 --> 00:23:49.139
policy in 2003, '(0)4 and '(0)5 did that.

00:23:49.139 --> 00:23:54.659
But I want, John-you can jump in after John
and then we'll open it up.

00:23:54.659 --> 00:23:56.139
LIPSKY: OK, OK.

00:23:56.139 --> 00:23:59.499
Look, let's look at this in a number of ways.

00:23:59.499 --> 00:24:06.109
If we're talking post crisis, post crisis
there has been a dramatic decline in leverage

00:24:06.109 --> 00:24:07.519
in the financial sector.

00:24:07.519 --> 00:24:08.519
Right?

00:24:08.519 --> 00:24:12.729
In fact, in the U.S. it's about by half and
in Europe nearly by half.

00:24:12.729 --> 00:24:18.059
So again, if we come back, have the changes,
the latest changes in the economic sector,

00:24:18.059 --> 00:24:22.389
the financial sector tended to retard credit
availability and growth?

00:24:22.389 --> 00:24:24.999
I would say yes, yeah.

00:24:24.999 --> 00:24:26.139
It seems pretty clear.

00:24:26.139 --> 00:24:30.440
And again, I come back to saying that doesn't
necessarily mean it's bad, but there certainly

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:36.070
has been a tradeoff between stability, enhancing
stability, and growth.

00:24:36.070 --> 00:24:41.821
The idea about that there hasn't been-that
everybody's been busy trading and no one's

00:24:41.821 --> 00:24:47.190
been paying any attention to the real economy,
geez, Joe, you used to be at Stanford.

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:49.149
You probably haven't been out there recently.

00:24:49.149 --> 00:24:50.149
STIGLITZ: No, I have.

00:24:50.149 --> 00:24:51.149
(Laughs.)

00:24:51.149 --> 00:24:52.149
LIPSKY: Oh, OK.

00:24:52.149 --> 00:24:54.830
Well, you go up on Sand Hill Road and you'll
see it's crawling-

00:24:54.830 --> 00:24:55.830
STIGLITZ: Small percentage.

00:24:55.830 --> 00:24:56.830
FOROOHAR: Tiny.

00:24:56.830 --> 00:24:57.830
STIGLITZ: Small percentage of the financial
sector.

00:24:57.830 --> 00:24:59.369
LIPSKY: There we go.

00:24:59.369 --> 00:25:00.369
But it's-yeah.

00:25:00.369 --> 00:25:01.369
STIGLITZ: It's important.

00:25:01.369 --> 00:25:02.369
LIPSKY: Thank you.

00:25:02.369 --> 00:25:03.369
Thank you.

00:25:03.369 --> 00:25:04.369
STIGLITZ: Exactly.

00:25:04.369 --> 00:25:05.369
But I would like to see a lot more Sand Hills.

00:25:05.369 --> 00:25:06.969
LIPSKY: Very good, very good.

00:25:06.969 --> 00:25:07.969
(Laughter.)

00:25:07.969 --> 00:25:11.929
And let's think about, what does the sector
really look like, the financial sector in

00:25:11.929 --> 00:25:13.599
the U.S.?

00:25:13.599 --> 00:25:19.729
The U.S. bond market is about $40 trillion
outstanding.

00:25:19.729 --> 00:25:24.429
Equity market's about 25 trillion (dollars)
outstanding.

00:25:24.429 --> 00:25:34.009
The U.S. investment managers of that control
about 45 trillion (dollars), and the banking

00:25:34.009 --> 00:25:36.690
sector about 18 trillion (dollars).

00:25:36.690 --> 00:25:38.759
So it's actually relatively small.

00:25:38.759 --> 00:25:44.899
So when we think about what are we talking
about in terms of who's doing what, it seems

00:25:44.899 --> 00:25:50.419
to me a lot of the discussion focuses on aspects
or should say global hedge funds.

00:25:50.419 --> 00:25:56.590
Globally hedge funds control about 2 1/2 trillion
(dollars), relatively small, small amount.

00:25:56.590 --> 00:26:02.509
So it seems to me a lot of the attention gets
focused not on the bread-and-butter and meat-and-potatoes.

00:26:02.509 --> 00:26:09.609
So what we've been lacking in this economy
has been not consumption demand, but investment,

00:26:09.609 --> 00:26:10.609
right?

00:26:10.609 --> 00:26:17.409
And right now, the market, not only does the
market send a signal to banks to say you are

00:26:17.409 --> 00:26:24.940
not currently investable, your price-to-book
is below 1, so why would I give my money to

00:26:24.940 --> 00:26:27.330
a bank so they can reduce value-added?

00:26:27.330 --> 00:26:36.379
And at the same time, it strikes me when you
look at the equity market more broadly, which

00:26:36.379 --> 00:26:42.739
has nothing to do with the banking system
directly, you can see that corporate management

00:26:42.739 --> 00:26:50.859
is being incentivized to conduct share buybacks
because that's what seems to get valued in

00:26:50.859 --> 00:26:51.899
the market.

00:26:51.899 --> 00:26:57.860
So it seems to me to say so this is a problem
that is caused by the financial sector.

00:26:57.860 --> 00:27:03.700
I'm certainly not trying to say there is no
problem that the sector can't be improved,

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:09.379
but I doubt if that is the primary locus of
the problems of this economy.

00:27:09.379 --> 00:27:11.899
FOROOHAR: Can I just follow up on a couple
of point?

00:27:11.899 --> 00:27:12.899
FISHER: Yes.

00:27:12.899 --> 00:27:15.419
FOROOHAR: I've also been really interested
in this idea that the largest banks are trading

00:27:15.419 --> 00:27:17.169
at relatively low valuations.

00:27:17.169 --> 00:27:23.059
You know, to me, in some ways, the markets
are actually giving a signal about the structure

00:27:23.059 --> 00:27:27.450
of banking, because if you look at what banking
analyst reports say, they would work better

00:27:27.450 --> 00:27:30.149
and be, you know, more highly valued if they
were broken up.

00:27:30.149 --> 00:27:34.690
Now, this is not a Bernie Sanders, you know,
break up the big banks, it's a silver bullet

00:27:34.690 --> 00:27:35.690
for getting rid of risk.

00:27:35.690 --> 00:27:37.539
We all know that that's not a silver bullet.

00:27:37.539 --> 00:27:41.679
And in fact, Joe did an amazing report for
the Roosevelt Institute on how there is no

00:27:41.679 --> 00:27:45.629
silver bullet because this is a 40-year process
that's been tiny, little, incremental changes,

00:27:45.629 --> 00:27:47.539
each of which require looking at.

00:27:47.539 --> 00:27:53.239
But I would also say that in my book and as
I think of finance, I'm looking not just at

00:27:53.239 --> 00:27:58.669
the formal banking sector, but at shadow banking,
at all the ways in which risk is moving into

00:27:58.669 --> 00:28:00.179
places that are even more opaque.

00:28:00.179 --> 00:28:02.399
I mean, I totally agree with you.

00:28:02.399 --> 00:28:08.000
Definitely Dodd-Frank has reduced risk and
leverage ratios in the formal banking sector,

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:10.509
but risk has gone to many other places.

00:28:10.509 --> 00:28:14.489
And what's really interesting to me is that
the corporate sector, I mean, the corporate

00:28:14.489 --> 00:28:18.860
bond market, the corporate junk bond market
has become a place where risk now lies.

00:28:18.860 --> 00:28:23.559
And big tech companies are underwriting corporate
bond offerings the way Goldman Sachs, you

00:28:23.559 --> 00:28:25.789
know, would, but they're not regulated in
the same way.

00:28:25.789 --> 00:28:27.960
So the entire-it is a larger ecosystem.

00:28:27.960 --> 00:28:32.460
And I do agree with you on the thing that
we spend way too much time, I think, in a

00:28:32.460 --> 00:28:36.970
really siloed conversation about, you know,
how much tier-one capital JPMorgan should

00:28:36.970 --> 00:28:37.970
be holding.

00:28:37.970 --> 00:28:42.259
We should be looking at a much bigger ecosystem
of finance and let's study where is the risk

00:28:42.259 --> 00:28:44.779
and what should the system be doing, per Joe's
point.

00:28:44.779 --> 00:28:45.779
LIPSKY: Sure.

00:28:45.779 --> 00:28:47.339
FISHER: Joe gets the last word before I open
it up to questions.

00:28:47.339 --> 00:28:48.339
LIPSKY: OK.

00:28:48.339 --> 00:28:49.339
(Laughs.)

00:28:49.339 --> 00:28:50.339
FISHER: I want to get one little bit.

00:28:50.339 --> 00:28:51.339
I know, you'll get quite a few.

00:28:51.339 --> 00:28:52.339
(Laughs.)

00:28:52.339 --> 00:28:56.549
STIGLITZ: So one way of thinking about it,
you know, go back sort of to textbooks economics.

00:28:56.549 --> 00:29:03.049
If you lower the cost of capital, you know,
monetary policy, the standard economics would

00:29:03.049 --> 00:29:09.299
be that lower cost of capital, that money
goes into more investment that stimulates

00:29:09.299 --> 00:29:11.559
economic growth.

00:29:11.559 --> 00:29:19.809
But between what the Fed does and Main Street
is the financial sector.

00:29:19.809 --> 00:29:25.710
And I'll use the financial sector in a very
broad term, like Rana, not just the banks,

00:29:25.710 --> 00:29:31.679
but a whole industry, OK, out there and some
of it even in the corporate sector when they

00:29:31.679 --> 00:29:32.679
engage.

00:29:32.679 --> 00:29:37.919
GE Capital was as much part of the financial
sector as any other.

00:29:37.919 --> 00:29:43.179
So I don't want to-it needs to be interpreted
in a very broad way.

00:29:43.179 --> 00:29:51.039
So you would have thought if we had a well-functioning
economy and a well-functioning financial sector,

00:29:51.039 --> 00:29:57.809
the low cost of capital would have stimulated
a lot of investment and that would be a period

00:29:57.809 --> 00:29:59.399
of high economic growth.

00:29:59.399 --> 00:30:06.669
There was nothing that said that that low
cost of capital would be translated into lots

00:30:06.669 --> 00:30:10.979
of consumption, you know, a housing bubble.

00:30:10.979 --> 00:30:16.809
But that's really where the debate, in some
sense, is.

00:30:16.809 --> 00:30:25.029
The central role of the financial sector is-two
of the central functions are allocating capital

00:30:25.029 --> 00:30:29.799
to create a productive economy, and managing
risk.

00:30:29.799 --> 00:30:32.779
And I'd say it failed on both.

00:30:32.779 --> 00:30:38.679
It allocated capital not to this productive
use that is part of the textbook, you go down

00:30:38.679 --> 00:30:45.249
to your investment banker and you have a lot
of investment, but to consumption and to existing

00:30:45.249 --> 00:30:48.719
assets creating asset bubbles.

00:30:48.719 --> 00:30:50.679
And that was not the attention in the Fed.

00:30:50.679 --> 00:30:56.869
I don't think-you know, I agree with you,
very critical of the Fed of its understanding

00:30:56.869 --> 00:31:03.969
that the translation of credit into productive
activities, that chain is a very weak chain

00:31:03.969 --> 00:31:09.080
and it could actually go the other way.

00:31:09.080 --> 00:31:13.580
And I think they still haven't fully appreciated
that.

00:31:13.580 --> 00:31:21.409
A second aspect of this that you often see,
you know, low interest rates, people say that

00:31:21.409 --> 00:31:28.200
causes more volatility because there's this
reach for yield and people put their money

00:31:28.200 --> 00:31:30.219
into more risky assets.

00:31:30.219 --> 00:31:40.339
In a standard, rational model that underlies
most of macroeconomics, that doesn't happen.

00:31:40.339 --> 00:31:46.590
You know, just because the interest rate is
lowered doesn't mean you act in a more reckless

00:31:46.590 --> 00:31:47.590
way.

00:31:47.590 --> 00:31:48.590
In fact, it should go the other way.

00:31:48.590 --> 00:31:53.330
So the bottom line is that our models of behavior
are wrong.

00:31:53.330 --> 00:31:56.779
I agree with you, macroeconomic policy is
wrong.

00:31:56.779 --> 00:32:04.039
But the financial sector has not guided either
the allocation of capital or the management

00:32:04.039 --> 00:32:08.700
of risk in ways that you would have hoped
it would have done to promote economic growth.

00:32:08.700 --> 00:32:15.330
So that period of low interest rates should
have been a period of enormous economic growth

00:32:15.330 --> 00:32:18.239
and stability, but it wasn't.

00:32:18.239 --> 00:32:19.239
FISHER: All right.

00:32:19.239 --> 00:32:21.639
I'm going to let the audience get in on the
action now, our members.

00:32:21.639 --> 00:32:26.859
I was remiss in not saying this is an on-the-record
session, but our guests all knew that in advance.

00:32:26.859 --> 00:32:28.080
FOROOHAR: Everything is on the record.

00:32:28.080 --> 00:32:31.379
FISHER: So now you know this was an on-the-record
session.

00:32:31.379 --> 00:32:34.350
And let me ask you to identify yourself.

00:32:34.350 --> 00:32:35.789
We have microphones roaming around.

00:32:35.789 --> 00:32:40.789
Identify yourself, pose your question quickly,
and then we'll try to have a dialogue.

00:32:40.789 --> 00:32:43.739
So here, right there, sir.

00:32:43.739 --> 00:32:47.349
Q: Thank you very much.

00:32:47.349 --> 00:32:48.799
Mahesh Kotecha.

00:32:48.799 --> 00:32:54.039
My question is regarding, you know, the problem
that John raised, which is, are we looking

00:32:54.039 --> 00:32:59.309
at the bread-and-butter as it were, the shadow
banking system versus the real banking system?

00:32:59.309 --> 00:33:02.619
The banking system relative to the shadow
banking system is actually not what it used

00:33:02.619 --> 00:33:03.619
to be.

00:33:03.619 --> 00:33:05.679
It is, as you pointed out, it's smaller.

00:33:05.679 --> 00:33:10.989
So the regulation typically goes to the regulated
side, missing the boat.

00:33:10.989 --> 00:33:12.450
What do you do about that?

00:33:12.450 --> 00:33:17.359
And are we successful in getting to the unregulated
sector which seems to be the tail that wags

00:33:17.359 --> 00:33:18.359
the dog?

00:33:18.359 --> 00:33:22.269
LIPSKY: That was just the point I was going
to make.

00:33:22.269 --> 00:33:23.269
(Laughter.)

00:33:23.269 --> 00:33:24.809
So you were-
FISHER: I was right.

00:33:24.809 --> 00:33:27.769
LIPSKY: You predicted accurately.

00:33:27.769 --> 00:33:33.409
Absolutely, the source of the problem, which
also reflected incentives, including government

00:33:33.409 --> 00:33:38.080
incentives, including a housing sector that
is probably the most distorted of any large

00:33:38.080 --> 00:33:43.889
economy in the world, and the response to
the crisis has been to pile more incentive

00:33:43.889 --> 00:33:50.779
into the housing sector, that the source was
not the regulated banking system, but the,

00:33:50.779 --> 00:34:00.419
let's call it, shadow banking and nonregulated
or more lightly regulated sector.

00:34:00.419 --> 00:34:07.330
Back at the IMF, we had five conclusions of
what needed to be done with regard to fixing

00:34:07.330 --> 00:34:08.849
the system.

00:34:08.849 --> 00:34:11.589
One was more capital, but two was to improve
regulations.

00:34:11.589 --> 00:34:17.809
And the primary problem that we saw with the
regulatory environment was a misdrawing of

00:34:17.809 --> 00:34:23.179
the perimeter of regulation that left systemically
important institutions outside the perimeter

00:34:23.179 --> 00:34:24.440
of regulation.

00:34:24.440 --> 00:34:31.039
And that has not been largely changed in that
almost all the regulation has focused so far

00:34:31.039 --> 00:34:32.139
on the banking system.

00:34:32.139 --> 00:34:39.080
And, Rana, one of the reasons why large banks
are poorly valued in the market is because

00:34:39.080 --> 00:34:45.299
they're in the process of attracting fantastic
capital charges that call into question their

00:34:45.299 --> 00:34:46.299
viability.

00:34:46.299 --> 00:34:51.269
It has nothing to do with the underlying business,
but has to do with regulatory decisions in

00:34:51.269 --> 00:34:59.089
which, for example, with TLAC coming up, total
loss-absorbing capital charges, the two banks

00:34:59.089 --> 00:35:04.500
in the world that sailed through the crisis
without batting an eye, never worrying for

00:35:04.500 --> 00:35:09.210
a minute that they were under threat, are
going to attract by far the highest charges.

00:35:09.210 --> 00:35:12.970
STIGLITZ: Can I say, this is an avenue where
John and I agree, it sounds like.

00:35:12.970 --> 00:35:13.970
(Laughter.)

00:35:13.970 --> 00:35:15.559
LIPSKY: I hope we agree on a lot, Joe.

00:35:15.559 --> 00:35:16.559
STIGLITZ: We agree.

00:35:16.559 --> 00:35:19.119
We do agree on a lot.

00:35:19.119 --> 00:35:23.990
And I just would raise two or three more points.

00:35:23.990 --> 00:35:29.620
One of them is, one has to ask the question,
why do we have a shadow banking system?

00:35:29.620 --> 00:35:34.790
And part of it is actually to circumvent the
regulations.

00:35:34.790 --> 00:35:40.319
And to the extent that that is true, we really
ought to be asking, you know, is the issue

00:35:40.319 --> 00:35:43.560
should we-we really ought to bring it all
in in some ways.

00:35:43.560 --> 00:35:49.500
It's a little bit like the question, you know,
an easier question is, why do we have offshore

00:35:49.500 --> 00:35:50.500
banking?

00:35:50.500 --> 00:35:57.450
You know, is it that finance really does better
in the Cayman Islands because of the sunshine?

00:35:57.450 --> 00:35:58.450
(Laughter.)

00:35:58.450 --> 00:36:00.829
You know, that makes money grow faster.

00:36:00.829 --> 00:36:03.309
Or is there something else going on?

00:36:03.309 --> 00:36:06.240
And we all know the answer to that.

00:36:06.240 --> 00:36:14.730
It's not like, you know, the expertise in
New York and in London isn't up to the expertise

00:36:14.730 --> 00:36:19.910
that they have in British Virgin Islands.

00:36:19.910 --> 00:36:20.910
So we know the answer.

00:36:20.910 --> 00:36:24.309
So the question is, why don't we just shut
it down?

00:36:24.309 --> 00:36:29.400
FISHER: We have a global collective action
problem is the answer.

00:36:29.400 --> 00:36:30.400
(Laughter.)

00:36:30.400 --> 00:36:32.410
Another question from the audience, who would
like to jump in?

00:36:32.410 --> 00:36:33.410
Yes, sir.

00:36:33.410 --> 00:36:35.030
Wait for the mic and identify yourself and
your affiliation.

00:36:35.030 --> 00:36:36.030
Q: Sorry.

00:36:36.030 --> 00:36:37.160
Jim Shapiro from the Tata Group.

00:36:37.160 --> 00:36:41.890
I just wonder if any of you would like to
comment on the failure of fiscal policy relative

00:36:41.890 --> 00:36:42.890
to all this.

00:36:42.890 --> 00:36:49.000
I mean, you mentioned the obvious role of
monetary policy, but one of the real failures,

00:36:49.000 --> 00:36:54.549
it seems to me, in terms of driving the investment,
much of which needed to be public investment

00:36:54.549 --> 00:37:00.170
or at least publicly led investment, is really
just a governmental failure in the U.S. and

00:37:00.170 --> 00:37:01.170
in other countries.

00:37:01.170 --> 00:37:04.710
And what part-how much does that sort of explain
some of what-

00:37:04.710 --> 00:37:07.609
FISHER: I'm going to intervene and translate
just because I want to see how Lipsky-(laughter)-will

00:37:07.609 --> 00:37:08.609
respond.

00:37:08.609 --> 00:37:11.849
FISHER: I have a shortcut to that.

00:37:11.849 --> 00:37:16.779
I've tried to teach my students at Dartmouth
without making them read the entire general

00:37:16.779 --> 00:37:17.819
theory.

00:37:17.819 --> 00:37:20.609
Why did Keynes become a Keynesian?

00:37:20.609 --> 00:37:24.260
Because he gave up on monetary policy.

00:37:24.260 --> 00:37:28.630
He didn't just from a vacuum decide the state
should do it, you know, because gee I can't

00:37:28.630 --> 00:37:29.920
think of anything else.

00:37:29.920 --> 00:37:34.410
He sat there in 1935 and '36 and said: Fiddling
with the rate of interest in order to simulate

00:37:34.410 --> 00:37:37.760
private credit to get investment going just
isn't going to do it.

00:37:37.760 --> 00:37:39.599
So let me see how they respond to that.

00:37:39.599 --> 00:37:43.500
STIGLITZ: Oh, I totally agree with that perspective.

00:37:43.500 --> 00:37:49.400
And the view-so the question, in a way, is
it is a failure of fiscal policy.

00:37:49.400 --> 00:37:54.281
They should have used even louder-they did
say this-that they should have used even louder

00:37:54.281 --> 00:37:57.089
the bully pulpit and said: We can't do it.

00:37:57.089 --> 00:37:59.339
It's really your responsibility.

00:37:59.339 --> 00:38:08.750
And that was a failure, not-there was a little
bit of, I think, a failure to do more about

00:38:08.750 --> 00:38:11.440
increasing the credit channel.

00:38:11.440 --> 00:38:17.760
The credit channel was broken and there was
more that they could have done to think about

00:38:17.760 --> 00:38:23.510
why it was broken and what could be done about
making it better.

00:38:23.510 --> 00:38:34.470
But the next question is, given that the fiscal
policy wasn't working, what should monetary

00:38:34.470 --> 00:38:36.529
policy have done?

00:38:36.529 --> 00:38:41.710
The first is making the credit channel work
better, but then the question is, say they

00:38:41.710 --> 00:38:43.529
can't do that.

00:38:43.529 --> 00:38:49.309
Should they have lowered the interest rates
to the level-are they having a productive

00:38:49.309 --> 00:38:50.970
or a counterproductive effect?

00:38:50.970 --> 00:38:54.960
And I think that's-you know, I think that's
open to debate.

00:38:54.960 --> 00:38:58.020
FISHER: Yes, ma'am.

00:38:58.020 --> 00:39:00.820
Microphone here, please.

00:39:00.820 --> 00:39:02.500
Q: I'm Lucy Komisar.

00:39:02.500 --> 00:39:03.500
I'm Lucy Komisar.

00:39:03.500 --> 00:39:04.500
I'm a journalist.

00:39:04.500 --> 00:39:08.599
And I've written for many years about the
offshore banking corporate secrecy system.

00:39:08.599 --> 00:39:11.560
So I'd like to ask Joe, how would you shut
it down?

00:39:11.560 --> 00:39:18.740
STIGLITZ: Well, in a way I'm-this picks up
a little bit on what John said and what we

00:39:18.740 --> 00:39:23.099
did in the Anti-Terror Act, you know.

00:39:23.099 --> 00:39:27.319
We did really-we cut off correspondence relationships.

00:39:27.319 --> 00:39:32.970
It had some adverse effects on some countries,
but it had-it worked.

00:39:32.970 --> 00:39:42.730
The system of financing for terrorism really-I
think it was an effective system.

00:39:42.730 --> 00:39:51.640
So if we had a resolve to do it, and there
would be a debate about the collateral damage

00:39:51.640 --> 00:39:58.890
and John would-(laughter)-I think it would
probably-it would be worth doing is precisely

00:39:58.890 --> 00:40:01.900
things like the correspondence banking.

00:40:01.900 --> 00:40:09.890
You say, lookit, if you're not willing to
sign up to these standards, you can't have

00:40:09.890 --> 00:40:11.319
correspondent relationships.

00:40:11.319 --> 00:40:12.799
FISHER: John?

00:40:12.799 --> 00:40:15.810
LIPSKY: Look, that's not really the problem,
Joe.

00:40:15.810 --> 00:40:21.539
The problem is you're responsible for your
correspondents living up to the standards

00:40:21.539 --> 00:40:23.400
the correspondent said.

00:40:23.400 --> 00:40:28.760
And you get this tremendous imbalance of risk
and reward, namely if your correspondent bank-the

00:40:28.760 --> 00:40:34.130
guy you've trusted actually fail, you're going
to get-you're going to get a huge-

00:40:34.130 --> 00:40:37.440
STIGLITZ: Yeah, but I'm answer his question
on how you do it.

00:40:37.440 --> 00:40:41.240
LIPSKY: Yeah, yeah, but there's another aspect
that's actually even more important.

00:40:41.240 --> 00:40:48.970
And that's what now goes under the BEPS, base
erosion profit shifting, that there is-the

00:40:48.970 --> 00:40:56.569
big money for tax authorities in governments
is not in stopping money laundering or financing

00:40:56.569 --> 00:40:57.569
of terrorism.

00:40:57.569 --> 00:41:04.700
It's really to make sure that you end up with
collecting the taxes that you think are due.

00:41:04.700 --> 00:41:08.440
And that-there's a big effort-you know this-there's
a big effort on that score.

00:41:08.440 --> 00:41:10.839
STIGLITZ: But that has not gone anywhere far
enough.

00:41:10.839 --> 00:41:14.089
I mean-
LIPSKY: But I think the idea of tax havens

00:41:14.089 --> 00:41:15.430
are disappearing.

00:41:15.430 --> 00:41:19.690
The notion, for instance, of Swiss bank secrecy
is long gone, et cetera, et cetera.

00:41:19.690 --> 00:41:20.950
So I think that's changed.

00:41:20.950 --> 00:41:24.829
Can I come for just a second to the question
on fiscal policy?

00:41:24.829 --> 00:41:26.109
Two things.

00:41:26.109 --> 00:41:31.880
I think in the immediate response to the crisis
the U.S. did a couple good things in terms

00:41:31.880 --> 00:41:33.130
of credit.

00:41:33.130 --> 00:41:39.120
TARP, which started out on a silly idea, the
troubled asset, right?

00:41:39.120 --> 00:41:41.109
They were going to buy back bad assets.

00:41:41.109 --> 00:41:43.490
In the end, they just said we're going to
recapitalize them.

00:41:43.490 --> 00:41:46.030
We're going to guarantee the recapitalization
of the banks.

00:41:46.030 --> 00:41:49.150
And then couple that with stress testing.

00:41:49.150 --> 00:41:50.789
That did a lot.

00:41:50.789 --> 00:41:53.200
What's missing?

00:41:53.200 --> 00:41:56.220
The common-the conventional wisdom is infrastructure.

00:41:56.220 --> 00:42:03.000
OK, yes, but my experience in my career is
to watch it.

00:42:03.000 --> 00:42:08.150
Just because somebody calls something an investment
doesn't mean it's really a good investment.

00:42:08.150 --> 00:42:11.400
And you can waste money on bad investments
big time-

00:42:11.400 --> 00:42:16.369
STIGLITZ: Like housing in Nevada in the desert,
like the private financial sector did?

00:42:16.369 --> 00:42:17.369
LIPSKY: Yeah.

00:42:17.369 --> 00:42:21.180
Well, you mean there was no public incentive
to-you know, in the housing sector?

00:42:21.180 --> 00:42:24.190
STIGLITZ: Nobody told them to invest in the
desert.

00:42:24.190 --> 00:42:29.250
LIPSKY: No, I'm not trying to say that nobody
made any mistakes, I'm just saying that let's

00:42:29.250 --> 00:42:33.730
not say just because something's infrastructure
that we should spend whatever.

00:42:33.730 --> 00:42:40.880
And then finally interest-I learned in public
finance 101, just today's interest rate is

00:42:40.880 --> 00:42:48.029
low doesn't mean you should engage in a 50-year
live asset based on today's rate, because

00:42:48.029 --> 00:42:53.349
you may actually-if this is distorted by public
policy-you may be effectively ripping off

00:42:53.349 --> 00:42:55.859
savers in the long run, which you may not
want to do.

00:42:55.859 --> 00:42:57.761
FOROOHAR: Can I-
FISHER: Rana wants to jump in.

00:42:57.761 --> 00:42:59.029
LIPSKY: That's not to say that more couldn't
have been done.

00:42:59.029 --> 00:43:02.029
And then finally tax reform is long overdue.

00:43:02.029 --> 00:43:03.029
FOROOHAR: Oh, yeah.

00:43:03.029 --> 00:43:04.280
Well, I was going to jump in on that too.

00:43:04.280 --> 00:43:05.630
I just want to say one thing on fiscal policy.

00:43:05.630 --> 00:43:08.799
I think it's worth pointing out that this
is a long-standing problem.

00:43:08.799 --> 00:43:10.650
This isn't even just a post-2008 problem.

00:43:10.650 --> 00:43:15.510
I actually looked in the first chapter in
my book at the ways in which at many points

00:43:15.510 --> 00:43:22.390
when there was political contentiousness and
disagreement about how to deal with an underlying

00:43:22.390 --> 00:43:28.430
growth problem-you know, from the 1970s onward,
really-that easy money, you know, pushing

00:43:28.430 --> 00:43:33.480
the ball to the market, saying let the markets
figure it out is a way that politicians have

00:43:33.480 --> 00:43:37.809
always managed these more difficult problems.

00:43:37.809 --> 00:43:39.510
So I think that's one important thing.

00:43:39.510 --> 00:43:43.400
The other thing-I just wanted to say, just
one kind of colorful story that I think speaks

00:43:43.400 --> 00:43:49.200
to the tax issues-the offshore tax issue,
and the tension points between finance and

00:43:49.200 --> 00:43:52.779
business, and really I think the Kafkaesque
way in which the markets have developed.

00:43:52.779 --> 00:43:54.400
If you just look at Apple, for example.

00:43:54.400 --> 00:43:59.890
Apple has about $2 billion sitting in bank
accounts, many of them offshore.

00:43:59.890 --> 00:44:04.109
It has made promises and issued I'm not sure
how many tens of billions of dollars of debt

00:44:04.109 --> 00:44:09.000
in the last three years-but it has made promises
to issue almost that amount of debt here at

00:44:09.000 --> 00:44:12.109
home in the bond markets, because it's cheaper,
obviously.

00:44:12.109 --> 00:44:14.470
And they don't want to pay the higher corporate
tax rate.

00:44:14.470 --> 00:44:18.860
That money is being paid out to, you know,
people-until recently, people like Carl Icahn,

00:44:18.860 --> 00:44:23.079
activist investors, the top ten percent of
the population that owns 80 percent of the

00:44:23.079 --> 00:44:24.079
stock.

00:44:24.079 --> 00:44:27.839
Which does to Joe's point about how financialization
increase inequality.

00:44:27.839 --> 00:44:31.440
And it's also just bizarre, because it makes
you wonder what are the capital markets for?

00:44:31.440 --> 00:44:35.930
I mean, corporations are involved in the capital
markets more than ever before, at a time when

00:44:35.930 --> 00:44:37.109
they don't actually need any capital, you
know?

00:44:37.109 --> 00:44:38.109
(Laughs.)

00:44:38.109 --> 00:44:42.559
Which is sort of a very question that I think
is important to keep in mind here.

00:44:42.559 --> 00:44:44.299
FISHER: Yes, Mr. Tempelsman.

00:44:44.299 --> 00:44:46.589
Here comes a microphone.

00:44:46.589 --> 00:44:49.529
Q: Maurice Tempelsman.

00:44:49.529 --> 00:44:52.680
Thank you very much for a good debate.

00:44:52.680 --> 00:45:00.809
One participant absent on this stage, if one
looks back at, say, the last 100 to 150 years,

00:45:00.809 --> 00:45:07.410
in this search for growth-motivation for growth
and stability, ranging all the way from state

00:45:07.410 --> 00:45:13.980
ownership to total laissez-faire, or triumphalism
of laissez-faire, the political factor has

00:45:13.980 --> 00:45:15.430
been totally omitted.

00:45:15.430 --> 00:45:22.029
And I think the interaction between the political-what
if feasible, what is doable-versus what is

00:45:22.029 --> 00:45:28.300
possibly desirable in an imperfect world is
something which is a very important-as we

00:45:28.300 --> 00:45:32.050
see it in our present political-(inaudible)-with
both the left and the right, with different

00:45:32.050 --> 00:45:39.119
methodology, different-really mining the same
political load.

00:45:39.119 --> 00:45:40.710
Can you comment on that?

00:45:40.710 --> 00:45:41.710
FISHER: Yeah.

00:45:41.710 --> 00:45:43.480
Can you say a word about that?

00:45:43.480 --> 00:45:44.480
LIPSKY: Yes.

00:45:44.480 --> 00:45:45.480
Real briefly.

00:45:45.480 --> 00:45:47.300
You've touched on something I feel very deeply
about.

00:45:47.300 --> 00:45:53.849
It seems to me that when the crisis hit in
2007, 2008, it produced collective action

00:45:53.849 --> 00:45:56.030
on a scale we've never seen before.

00:45:56.030 --> 00:46:00.210
The principal institutional response to the
crisis was the formation of the G-20 at the

00:46:00.210 --> 00:46:01.859
leaders' level.

00:46:01.859 --> 00:46:08.910
And the cooperation shown, for example, between
November 2008 and April 2009, the London summit,

00:46:08.910 --> 00:46:15.279
I think everybody agrees had a tremendously
positive effect on ameliorating the downturn

00:46:15.279 --> 00:46:17.520
and setting the stage for an upturn.

00:46:17.520 --> 00:46:23.990
I, for one, hope that that would be a lesson
learned that would produce a much more cooperative

00:46:23.990 --> 00:46:29.220
and collaborative approach to macroeconomic
policy setting that was originally established

00:46:29.220 --> 00:46:33.819
in Pittsburgh in September of 2009 in the
formation of what we called the framework

00:46:33.819 --> 00:46:39.509
for strong, sustainable, and balanced growth-an
initiative that I thought showed tremendous

00:46:39.509 --> 00:46:45.410
progress in the idea of collaboration, not
I'll sacrifice for the greater good, but that

00:46:45.410 --> 00:46:51.069
if we all work together we can produce, create
a superior result that leaves everybody better-at

00:46:51.069 --> 00:46:54.579
least as well off or better off than before.

00:46:54.579 --> 00:46:58.890
And how much have you heard about this effort
from the political leaders?

00:46:58.890 --> 00:47:03.599
It's still going on at a technical level,
but there's just been no political follow

00:47:03.599 --> 00:47:04.599
through.

00:47:04.599 --> 00:47:06.250
And I've been bitterly disappointed with that.

00:47:06.250 --> 00:47:08.059
STIGLITZ: Yeah, and can I just say one word
about that?

00:47:08.059 --> 00:47:13.019
And that goes back to the macro or the fiscal.

00:47:13.019 --> 00:47:21.859
The view, particularly of Germany, that austerity
is the solution has really led to-I think

00:47:21.859 --> 00:47:27.590
played an important role in undermining the
ability to reach a coordinated solution, because

00:47:27.590 --> 00:47:38.109
there are just very deep political divide-philosophical
divide on what is necessary to get cooperative

00:47:38.109 --> 00:47:42.579
action that would lead to growth for the global
economy.

00:47:42.579 --> 00:47:44.710
FISHER: Yes, sir.

00:47:44.710 --> 00:47:48.269
Here in the red tie.

00:47:48.269 --> 00:47:49.960
Here comes the mic.

00:47:49.960 --> 00:47:52.819
Q: Ed Cox, New York State Republican Party
and director of Noble Energy.

00:47:52.819 --> 00:47:57.869
In the upstream oil-or taking the sand hill
argument one step further-in the upstream

00:47:57.869 --> 00:48:05.210
oil and gas sector, I have seen the availability
of equity, bond markets hedging very important

00:48:05.210 --> 00:48:07.819
recently, doing strategic mergers.

00:48:07.819 --> 00:48:15.390
The financial sector has really permitted
the upstream oil and gas sector to drive innovation

00:48:15.390 --> 00:48:18.749
and produce cheap oil and gas.

00:48:18.749 --> 00:48:25.299
That has been really tremendous impact-positive
impact for our economy, and frankly the world

00:48:25.299 --> 00:48:26.299
economy.

00:48:26.299 --> 00:48:32.320
So where has the financial sector gone wrong
with respect to that important area of our

00:48:32.320 --> 00:48:33.339
economy?

00:48:33.339 --> 00:48:35.400
STIGLITZ: OK.

00:48:35.400 --> 00:48:38.170
Well, let me just say something.

00:48:38.170 --> 00:48:39.470
It may-
LIPSKY: (Laughs.)

00:48:39.470 --> 00:48:40.470
About fossil fuels?

00:48:40.470 --> 00:48:45.579
STIGLITZ: Well, there are two things-two things
where I think there's a problem.

00:48:45.579 --> 00:48:55.230
One was the question of did they overinvest
in the same way they overinvested in houses

00:48:55.230 --> 00:48:57.289
in the Nevada desert?

00:48:57.289 --> 00:49:02.650
And what we now know is that almost surely
they did.

00:49:02.650 --> 00:49:06.039
And there's a wave of bankruptcies going on.

00:49:06.039 --> 00:49:13.410
So from that-even from that narrow point of
view, there was not management of the allocation

00:49:13.410 --> 00:49:14.430
of resources.

00:49:14.430 --> 00:49:19.490
It was important-OK, I know you'll disagree.

00:49:19.490 --> 00:49:30.450
The second one is, the oil and gas market
does not reflect the real social cost of carbon-of

00:49:30.450 --> 00:49:34.680
carbon emissions, which is very, very high.

00:49:34.680 --> 00:49:43.450
And therefore, there's been overexpansion
of that sector relative to renewable energy.

00:49:43.450 --> 00:49:46.921
So in my mind-but that, I don't blame the
financial sector.

00:49:46.921 --> 00:49:50.490
FOROOHAR: I would-
STIGLITZ: But that is public policy.

00:49:50.490 --> 00:49:56.430
We should have had a public policy to deal
with the social cost of carbon.

00:49:56.430 --> 00:50:01.269
And they're responding to-so I don't blame
the financial sector for that at all.

00:50:01.269 --> 00:50:06.710
They're just responding to the failure of
government to-you know, or collective action

00:50:06.710 --> 00:50:09.161
to-
FOROOHAR: Also, the commodities sector, one

00:50:09.161 --> 00:50:13.049
of the reasons it's so volatile is it's become
one of the most highly financialized sectors.

00:50:13.049 --> 00:50:19.690
I mean, you know, a lot of-a lot of money
has gone into commodities-raw commodities

00:50:19.690 --> 00:50:21.619
or plays on commodities.

00:50:21.619 --> 00:50:23.190
And you see that flowing out.

00:50:23.190 --> 00:50:25.700
And I mean, you know, hedge funders themselves
would say that.

00:50:25.700 --> 00:50:28.070
There's plenty of Senate testimony on the
topic.

00:50:28.070 --> 00:50:32.380
LIPSKY: Yeah, although outside of energy the
size of the commodity markets is much smaller

00:50:32.380 --> 00:50:33.599
than people understand it.

00:50:33.599 --> 00:50:37.890
STIGLITZ: But let me just raise one question
that Mark Carney has raised, who's the head

00:50:37.890 --> 00:50:40.819
of the Bank of England, and I agree.

00:50:40.819 --> 00:50:50.050
And that is, embedded in our current financial
market is a contagion risk that we haven't

00:50:50.050 --> 00:50:59.000
really, fully taken into account, that if-we
ought to be thinking, what will happen if

00:50:59.000 --> 00:51:08.049
the-we finally do put a price of carbon, or
we recognize that, you know, global warming

00:51:08.049 --> 00:51:10.119
is really serious?

00:51:10.119 --> 00:51:13.359
Then the price of those carbon assets are
going to go down.

00:51:13.359 --> 00:51:20.039
You know, there will be these calculations
that a very large fraction of reserves are

00:51:20.039 --> 00:51:22.549
going to have to stay in the ground.

00:51:22.549 --> 00:51:33.490
And if you look at the network-the financial
network, firms that own assets that own assets

00:51:33.490 --> 00:51:40.230
that are related to the financial sector,
it is-there is a systemic problem of a very

00:51:40.230 --> 00:51:43.970
significant order of magnitude that has not
been fully incorporated-

00:51:43.970 --> 00:51:49.119
LIPSKY: Right, but, Joe, let me say, you can
be sure that senior management of large financial

00:51:49.119 --> 00:51:52.640
institutions that have long-lived assets are
thinking hard about this topic, absolutely.

00:51:52.640 --> 00:51:53.640
STIGLITZ: Yeah.

00:51:53.640 --> 00:51:54.640
Yeah.

00:51:54.640 --> 00:51:56.390
FISHER: Question in the back?

00:51:56.390 --> 00:51:58.510
Yes, ma'am.

00:51:58.510 --> 00:52:01.260
Q: Bhakti Mirchandani, One William Street.

00:52:01.260 --> 00:52:02.740
Thank you for a wonderful discussion.

00:52:02.740 --> 00:52:07.279
Earlier in the discussion, there was talk
of the perimeter of regulation being too narrow.

00:52:07.279 --> 00:52:11.839
What would you ideally like to see in terms
of the systemically important institutions

00:52:11.839 --> 00:52:12.950
that are outside that perimeter?

00:52:12.950 --> 00:52:15.160
How would you like to see them regulated?

00:52:15.160 --> 00:52:20.720
LIPSKY: Well, without getting specific about
how do you regulate the insurance business,

00:52:20.720 --> 00:52:22.480
how do you-et cetera.

00:52:22.480 --> 00:52:31.599
But essentially, what we saw was the ability
to collect-to create pools of leverage outside

00:52:31.599 --> 00:52:35.069
of the regulatory system.

00:52:35.069 --> 00:52:39.410
AIGFP, Countrywide Finance, et cetera.

00:52:39.410 --> 00:52:42.609
And it seems to me that there needs to-we
need two things.

00:52:42.609 --> 00:52:49.900
We need to find a way to make sure that these
sources of leverage are fairly regulated,

00:52:49.900 --> 00:52:55.640
or else you've got exactly what Rana was talking
about, is the risk will move to the less regulated

00:52:55.640 --> 00:53:01.240
spot and it can become excessive and distorted.

00:53:01.240 --> 00:53:08.059
Secondly, an aspect I remarked, but I think
has been underappreciated, is how much the

00:53:08.059 --> 00:53:18.140
post-crisis regulation has tended to be based
on national boundaries, and how the initial

00:53:18.140 --> 00:53:26.049
effort to create a level playing field by
regulatory reform, I would say, has not succeeded.

00:53:26.049 --> 00:53:33.579
And as a result, has become a barrier to,
I think, the more efficient development of

00:53:33.579 --> 00:53:34.820
cross-border flows.

00:53:34.820 --> 00:53:40.869
And hence, it is not the only reason, but
it is not so surprising that for the first

00:53:40.869 --> 00:53:48.539
time since World War II, since the crisis
global trade has grown more slowly than global

00:53:48.539 --> 00:53:49.839
GDP.

00:53:49.839 --> 00:53:54.880
And I think that's a sign that shouldn't be
just excused away.

00:53:54.880 --> 00:53:56.529
And I think there's some cause and effect
here.

00:53:56.529 --> 00:53:57.529
STIGLITZ: Yeah.

00:53:57.529 --> 00:54:01.740
Although, let me just say, from a longer-term
perspective, as we move more to a service

00:54:01.740 --> 00:54:06.059
sector economy, I would have expected that
to happen.

00:54:06.059 --> 00:54:11.250
So again, many factors and it's correlation
versus causation.

00:54:11.250 --> 00:54:14.529
FOROOHAR: And digital trade flows are actually
exponentially growing.

00:54:14.529 --> 00:54:16.990
It's real, you know, physical goods.

00:54:16.990 --> 00:54:18.749
So, can I just add one thing to this?

00:54:18.749 --> 00:54:21.910
Which is, you know, I'm not an expert in regulation,
and I won't pretend to be.

00:54:21.910 --> 00:54:26.600
But I think an interesting question would
be also how to incentivize the behaviors that

00:54:26.600 --> 00:54:31.240
we want, versus just regulating the behaviors
that we don't want.

00:54:31.240 --> 00:54:35.839
It has always seemed to me that, you know,
the basic question is, is an institution,

00:54:35.839 --> 00:54:38.079
is a company providing a clear, measurable
good?

00:54:38.079 --> 00:54:40.329
And if they are, let's reward them for that.

00:54:40.329 --> 00:54:41.329
Let's incentivize that.

00:54:41.329 --> 00:54:45.490
And that might go to some broader conversations
about the tax code and how it subsidizes debt

00:54:45.490 --> 00:54:47.489
and leverage, versus equity and savings.

00:54:47.489 --> 00:54:49.559
FISHER: Yes, ma'am.

00:54:49.559 --> 00:54:50.849
Last question.

00:54:50.849 --> 00:54:52.289
LIPSKY: Aww.

00:54:52.289 --> 00:54:55.880
Q: Pat Cloherty, venture capitalist.

00:54:55.880 --> 00:55:04.400
What would you think of the idea that excessive
regulation, in many ways, provides the basis

00:55:04.400 --> 00:55:05.550
for growth?

00:55:05.550 --> 00:55:11.520
And having been in this business 45 years,
I've watched AT&amp;T be dismantled, all kinds

00:55:11.520 --> 00:55:17.849
of regulation there, and the downstream consequences
for innovation in telecommunications were

00:55:17.849 --> 00:55:21.359
vast, for an unregulated-then-unregulated
sector.

00:55:21.359 --> 00:55:24.869
Likewise, IBM-the breakup of IBM.

00:55:24.869 --> 00:55:30.859
So we get the downsizing of computers, so
there were antitrust, all kinds of regulations.

00:55:30.859 --> 00:55:37.470
So is it perverse to think that maybe extending
the boundary of bank regulation would not

00:55:37.470 --> 00:55:38.470
be a good idea?

00:55:38.470 --> 00:55:43.910
FISHER: Can we ask you each to take a bite
at this, and each get a last word in?

00:55:43.910 --> 00:55:45.749
And then we'll end.

00:55:45.749 --> 00:55:46.749
So, John.

00:55:46.749 --> 00:55:47.749
LIPSKY: OK.

00:55:47.749 --> 00:55:49.480
Yes, that is definitely a risk.

00:55:49.480 --> 00:55:54.859
That I wanted to point out, there's a big
difference, for example, between the Japanese,

00:55:54.859 --> 00:55:59.509
European economies on one hand and the U.S.
economy on the other in terms of the relative

00:55:59.509 --> 00:56:06.420
role of regulated finance-i.e., banks, commercial
banks, as a source of funding-versus more

00:56:06.420 --> 00:56:09.880
market-driven securities markets.

00:56:09.880 --> 00:56:15.079
And what you see, and it's, of course, post-crisis,
that the securities markets have driven a

00:56:15.079 --> 00:56:16.960
cleaning of the balance sheet.

00:56:16.960 --> 00:56:22.109
It doesn't prevent mistakes, but when mistakes
are made they get recognized and dealt with

00:56:22.109 --> 00:56:23.900
very quickly, then you move on.

00:56:23.900 --> 00:56:28.339
Whereas, there has been a tendency in the
much more highly regulated commercial banking

00:56:28.339 --> 00:56:33.329
system, they're very slow to recognize losses,
very slow to create realistic valuations.

00:56:33.329 --> 00:56:37.819
And that has clearly inhibited growth to the
point that the U.S. economy is now-what, GDP's,

00:56:37.819 --> 00:56:40.869
what, 10 percent above where it was pre-crisis?

00:56:40.869 --> 00:56:45.309
And in the European Union, they are just back
to pre-crisis levels now.

00:56:45.309 --> 00:56:47.380
And I-
FISHER: Joe, your last word.

00:56:47.380 --> 00:56:52.660
STIGLITZ: Well, the last point, I guess I
can't help-(inaudible)-(laughter)-the main

00:56:52.660 --> 00:56:57.569
reason for that is the euro-I have a book
coming out, and I shouldn't mention this,

00:56:57.569 --> 00:57:01.450
but on the devastating effect of the euro.

00:57:01.450 --> 00:57:04.970
And that's the real reason for the problems
in Europe.

00:57:04.970 --> 00:57:11.239
The broader point that you made, which is
that there are often, almost always unintended

00:57:11.239 --> 00:57:20.809
consequences that you can't fully take into
account, when we look at that particular experiment,

00:57:20.809 --> 00:57:27.950
breaking up AT&amp;T, we have to remember that
AT&amp;T was very important in bringing the transistor,

00:57:27.950 --> 00:57:31.440
a lot of innovations, basic research.

00:57:31.440 --> 00:57:37.819
It wasn't very good at bringing the last mile,
bringing the goods to the market.

00:57:37.819 --> 00:57:41.210
But in terms of basic research, it was phenomenal.

00:57:41.210 --> 00:57:47.440
It had more Nobel Prizes than any country,
outside the United States.

00:57:47.440 --> 00:57:50.520
So the point is that was an unintended consequence.

00:57:50.520 --> 00:57:54.720
I think there's been a slowdown of some of
the basic research, as a result of that.

00:57:54.720 --> 00:57:56.380
Nobody intended that.

00:57:56.380 --> 00:58:10.180
The broader view-my last word-is just to reiterate
what Rana said, that our major challenge is

00:58:10.180 --> 00:58:15.619
to try to figure out how to make sure that
our financial system does what it's supposed

00:58:15.619 --> 00:58:16.619
to do.

00:58:16.619 --> 00:58:22.210
And too much of our discussion has been trying
to prevent the problems of the things that

00:58:22.210 --> 00:58:23.640
it's gone wrong.

00:58:23.640 --> 00:58:24.640
You know, we have to do that.

00:58:24.640 --> 00:58:27.660
You know, we have to stop all the bad things.

00:58:27.660 --> 00:58:31.039
But in all that discussion, there's just been
two little questions.

00:58:31.039 --> 00:58:34.059
What are the core functions?

00:58:34.059 --> 00:58:40.430
What would a banking system-or, not a banking
system-a financial system that really fulfills

00:58:40.430 --> 00:58:41.430
that?

00:58:41.430 --> 00:58:48.950
There's been a little bit too much faith,
in this discussion between markets and institutions,

00:58:48.950 --> 00:58:52.710
markets and banks, a little bit too much faith,
I think, in markets.

00:58:52.710 --> 00:58:56.240
I think institutions like banks are really
important.

00:58:56.240 --> 00:59:00.079
They're good at information, theoretic reasoning
for that.

00:59:00.079 --> 00:59:07.589
So I think we've lost sight of the bigger
picture, and that as we begin-continuing our

00:59:07.589 --> 00:59:13.579
discussion of reform-regulatory reform, I
think we ought to put a lot more emphasis

00:59:13.579 --> 00:59:14.829
on the bigger picture.

00:59:14.829 --> 00:59:15.989
FISHER: You get the last, Rana.

00:59:15.989 --> 00:59:21.789
FOROOHAR: I will just take a kind of existential
step back and just say: I think de-siloing

00:59:21.789 --> 00:59:26.140
the debate, realizing that making a healthy
capital market system is not just about formal

00:59:26.140 --> 00:59:31.180
banking, it's also shadow banking, it's tax
policy, it's housing policy, it's retirement

00:59:31.180 --> 00:59:32.180
policy and pensions.

00:59:32.180 --> 00:59:35.329
We haven't even gotten into that whole topic.

00:59:35.329 --> 00:59:39.460
And that it has to really be about all those
pieces, because they interact together.

00:59:39.460 --> 00:59:45.309
And I also think that just changing the narrative-I
feel like there's been a sense that finance

00:59:45.309 --> 00:59:48.809
was the kind of tippy-top of the services
economy pyramid that we're all supposed to

00:59:48.809 --> 00:59:49.809
aspire to.

00:59:49.809 --> 00:59:54.079
You know, after you graduate from being agrarian
to a manufacturing economy to a services economy?

00:59:54.079 --> 00:59:55.840
And I would actually switch it.

00:59:55.840 --> 01:00:00.559
I mean, it's called financial services for
a reason, because finance is supposed to serve

01:00:00.559 --> 01:00:01.559
business.

01:00:01.559 --> 01:00:03.859
FISHER: Thank my trio, and thank the members
for coming.

01:00:03.859 --> 01:00:04.859
Thank you all.

01:00:04.859 --> 01:00:05.859
(Applause.)

01:00:05.859 --> 01:00:05.860
(END)

